  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Secretary of State Rubio's June 2 Senate testimony — describing Iranian mining of "large segments" of the Strait of Hormuz and the IRGC "firing on commercial ships" — constitutes the clearest US government acknowledgment to date that Iran's actions have crossed into active interdiction of commercial navigation. UKMTO JMIC Advisory Note 005-26, confirmed as observed on June 5, independently corroborates ongoing mining activity. The Panaya tanker strike, reported by Iran Focus and corroborated by contextual TIER1 reporting of IRGC naval activity, adds a second vector of commercial-vessel targeting. These developments satisfy the evidentiary threshold for a meaningful probability increase on <span class="cite">F1</span> from 40% to 50%. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>The Iran-US MOU deadline passed without resolution today. FM Araghchi's June 4 statement of "no significant progress" (CNN) — a public correction of Trump's claim that talks resumed "at a rapid pace" — closes <span class="cite">F2</span> as RESOLVED-NO. <span class="cite">F3</span> resolves simultaneously: with live trackers showing approximately 4 commercial vessels per day and zero outbound commercial transits on multiple early-June days, the 50-vessel 7-day threshold required by June 10 is structurally unreachable. Both resolutions leave the diplomatic and maritime recovery tracks in the same impasse that has characterized the conflict since Day 70. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span>, → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The IRGC attacks on June 2–3 — drone and missile strikes on Kuwait's international airport and the US Fifth Fleet headquarters in Bahrain — represent a qualitative escalation beyond Hormuz interdiction into direct strikes on US military infrastructure and allied civilian infrastructure. These strikes are consistent with a deliberate hardliner-faction choice that the IRGC, not Pezeshkian's diplomatic line, holds operational authority. Iran International's May 31 report that Pezeshkian offered his resignation due to IRGC "complete takeover" of decision-making (currently TIER2, unconfirmed) aligns with this pattern. If confirmed by a TIER1 source, <span class="cite">F4</span> and <span class="cite">F8</span> should be revised downward materially. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move, → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>The July 2 Hormuz recovery forecast absorbs the full impact of the June 2–3 escalation. The path to 18% on <span class="cite">F5</span> requires an MOU signed within days, immediate demining commencement by NAVCENT-authorized parties, and restoration of carrier confidence — a chain of dependent steps that has become significantly more difficult given fresh UKMTO mine-laying advisories, elevated Lloyd's war-risk premiums, and no carrier reversals. The −12pp move from 30% to 18% is the direct consequence of near-eliminating the optimistic scenario in which F2 success could have initiated a rapid recovery timeline. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:45%;">Escalation 45%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:40%;">Protracted 40%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:15%;">De-esc 15%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (45%):</strong> Full formal Hormuz closure (F1=50%) and IRGC attacks on US Fifth Fleet headquarters create a direct path to wider US-Iran military exchange if a US counter-strike triggers further IRGC retaliation.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (40%):</strong> The most probable path is continued IRGC harassment of commercial shipping, inconclusive diplomatic engagement via Pakistan and Omani channels, and a frozen impasse in which neither a deal nor a decisive military confrontation materializes within the June–July window.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (15%):</strong> De-escalation requires an MOU signed within days (F8=25%), immediate demining, and carrier confidence restoration — a sequentially dependent chain that becomes less viable with each additional IRGC escalation action.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>The internal Iranian power configuration that matters most for all active forecasts is the relationship between Pezeshkian's presidency and the IRGC command structure aligned with Mojtaba Khamenei. The June 2–3 retaliatory strikes on Kuwait and Bahrain were announced as Iranian government responses to US self-defense strikes near Hormuz — but their targeting of allied civilian infrastructure (Kuwait airport) and US military headquarters represented a deliberate escalation choice that Pezeshkian's diplomatic line would not have selected. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Iran International's May 31 report that Pezeshkian offered his resignation due to IRGC "complete takeover" of decision-making has not been confirmed by Reuters, AP, or AFP as of June 5. It remains TIER2 and is flagged for human verification. If confirmed, the practical consequence is that Araghchi's public statements — including his "no significant progress" correction of Trump on June 4 — come from a negotiating line whose authority to bind the Iranian state is nominal. The IRGC/Khamenei channel would then become the only track capable of delivering a durable agreement, and that channel has shown no diplomatic engagement. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move, → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Araghchi's June 4 framing ("no significant progress" rather than "talks terminated") is the thin reed on which <span class="cite">F4</span> remains at 45% rather than lower. It preserves the diplomatic fiction of ongoing engagement, which is a necessary but not sufficient condition for actual re-engagement. The Pakistan mediator channel (FM Dar's June 4 Washington meetings with Rubio) represents third-party activity but requires Iranian MFA acknowledgment to constitute evidence of authorized back-channel contact. Until IRNA or Tasnim confirms Pakistani mediation as an authorized track, this remains context-level. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

